From: perf session 2026-09-11
Subject: [PATCH 0024] pmb887x: dmac/dif/ssc completion timers on QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL (stock icount)

The DMA controller and the display/serial interfaces complete a transfer
from a timer armed at "now" (+1 ns for the dmac re-arm) on
QEMU_CLOCK_REALTIME, i.e. on the main-loop thread: the display-DMA stretch
(~12k single-word transfers, one IRQ + WFI per word) paid a main-loop round
trip per word.  On QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL the vCPU thread runs the timer itself
(icount_handle_deadline at the next TB boundary, or the first warp of
0023's idle loop when the guest is already in WFI) - deterministic,
instruction-proportional completion instead of a host-latency race.
The same change was measured neutral before 0023 (playbook REJECTED row,
now superseded): the hop chain was identical for both clocks then.

Not under the opt-in icount2 model (?icount=precise-clocks): there a due
virtual timer runs synchronously inside timer_mod (timerlist_rearm ->
icount2_sync -> qemu_clock_run_timers), i.e. re-entrantly from the device
callback that armed it (dmac_schedule inside dmac_channel_run) - the first
build stalled that mode at v=7.2; pmb887x_completion_clock() keeps the
realtime clock when icount2 is enabled.

Measured on top of 0023 (idlebench --quick, one pair each):
  dist:     t0.75G 49.2->47.0 (-4 %), t1.2G 59.7->57.5 (-4 %)
  dist-jit: t0.75G 45.3->44.6 (-2 %), t1.3G 49.2->48.2 (-2 %)
  (the 0.5G->0.75G display stretch: dist 15.2->12.5 s, dist-jit 11.1->9.1 s)
Gates: see 0023 (stack); precise-clocks boot re-checked (v=46 at 40 s, as base).

diff --git a/hw/arm/pmb887x/dif_v1.c b/hw/arm/pmb887x/dif_v1.c
